{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And Esav went to Yishmael.</b> Our Rabbis taught in [tractate] HaHovel. People say: a bad palm — [it stays] among a thicket of ferns. Rashi explained: barren oaks that bear no fruit; like a barren man — worthless. That is to say, it is the way of a bad palm to grow beside barren trees. This matter is written in the Torah: 'And Esav went to Yishmael.' It is repeated in the Prophets: 'And they gathered to Yiftach — empty men.' And a third time in the Writings: 'Every bird [of] its kind shall dwell, and a man with his likeness.' They even call the book of Ben Sira 'Writings.' <b>And he took Mahalat daughter of Yishmael.</b> Esav said: in these latter blessings Isaac gave Jacob the land of Canaan that was given to Abraham, and commanded him to take a wife from his family and not from the daughters of Canaan. 'What I married from the daughters of Canaan has cut me off from the land of Canaan' — he went and took for himself the daughter of Yishmael. He said: perhaps from now on I will merit the inheritance of Abraham." "<b>Sister of Nevayot.</b> The eldest among his brothers. <b>The sedra of parashat Toledot is completed.</b>"], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {0 "The Aramaic proverb 'גברא שרכא' is rendered literally as 'a barren man'; the word can denote a palm that bears no fruit or a sterile person, both senses operative here."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:06:29.853626171Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}